(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[4] = {"grade":4,"packs":{"grade4-english":{"g4eng-nouns":[{"id":"g4eng-cover-abstract-04","q":"Which word is an abstract noun in this sentence: \"Honesty can make a difficult task easier.\"","opts":["task","easier","honesty","difficult"],"a":"honesty","hint":"An abstract noun names something you cannot usually touch or hold.","exp":"<b>honesty</b> is a quality; you cannot hold it in your hand, so it is an abstract noun."},{"id":"g4eng-cover-gender-06","q":"Which word is the feminine form of \"brother\"?","opts":["brother","person","brothers","sister"],"a":"sister","hint":"Some nouns have a masculine and a feminine form.","exp":"<b>sister</b> is the feminine form of <b>brother</b>."},{"id":"g4eng-cover-art-12","q":"Which phrase uses the correct indefinite article before \"book\"?","opts":["some book","an book","the book always","a book"],"a":"a book","hint":"Say both articles aloud before the word.","exp":"The correct phrase is <b>a book</b>. The choice depends on the sound at the beginning of the word, not only the first letter."},{"id":"g4eng-noun-010","q":"Read this passage: \"Asha found a puppy near the school gate. She brought it home and fed it some milk. Her parents were surprised when they saw it.\" Which pronoun refers to ASHA'S PARENTS?","opts":["it","they","Her","She"],"a":"they","hint":"Find who \"they\" refers to - which noun in the passage comes before this pronoun?","exp":"\"<b>They</b>\" refers to Asha's parents - \"they saw it\" means her parents saw the puppy. Tracking what each pronoun refers back to (its antecedent) is a key comprehension skill. \"She\" = Asha. \"It\" = the puppy. \"Her\" = Asha (possessive)."},{"id":"g4eng-noun-019","q":"Read: \"Every evening, the family gathers in their kitchen. Grandmother prepares dal and rice while the children do homework.\" How many COMMON nouns are in these two sentences?","opts":["7","8","6","5"],"a":"7","hint":"Common nouns are general naming words (not specific names of people or places). Find all nouns, then remove any proper nouns.","exp":"The 7 common nouns are: <b>evening, family, kitchen, dal, rice, children, homework</b>. \"Grandmother\" is capitalised here and used as a title/name (a proper noun). \"Their\" is a pronoun. Identifying common nouns from a passage is a key MIE Grade 4 grammar skill."}],"g4eng-verbs":[{"id":"g4eng-verb-017","q":"Which sentence uses the SIMPLE PRESENT tense to express a permanent fact?","opts":["The sun rose in the east.","The sun rises in the east.","The sun will rise in the east.","The sun is rising in the east."],"a":"The sun rises in the east.","hint":"For permanent facts and general truths, we use the simple present tense.","exp":"\"<b>The sun rises in the east</b>\" - the simple present expresses permanent facts and general truths (always true). \"Rises\" is the third person singular form (he/she/it + verb+s)."},{"id":"g4eng-cover-verb-context-04","q":"Read: \"Our neighbours are painting their gate.\" Which word is a verb?","opts":["the","at","painting","safely"],"a":"painting","hint":"A verb shows an action or a state of being.","exp":"<b>painting</b> is the action word, so it is a verb."},{"id":"g4eng-hd-016","q":"Mira said: <i>\"I am cooking dholl puri.\"</i> Which sentence reports what she said?","opts":["Mira said that she cooked the dholl puri.","Mira said that she has been cooking dholl puri.","Mira said that she was cooking dholl puri.","Mira said that she is cooking dholl puri."],"a":"Mira said that she was cooking dholl puri.","hint":"\"Said\" has already moved into the past. The rest of the sentence follows it.","exp":"When the reporting word moves back (\"said\"), the verb inside moves back with it: \"am cooking\" becomes <b>was cooking</b>. \"Cooked\" would lose the sense that the cooking was going on at the time."},{"id":"g4eng-verb-009","q":"Which sentence uses verbs CORRECTLY throughout?","opts":["Yesterday, I was go to the shop and buy milk.","Yesterday, I gone to the shop and buyed milk.","Yesterday, I go to the shop and buys milk.","Yesterday, I went to the shop and bought milk."],"a":"Yesterday, I went to the shop and bought milk.","hint":"\"Yesterday\" tells you both actions happened in the past. Both verbs must be in the past tense.","exp":"\"<b>Went</b>\" and \"<b>bought</b>\" are both correct irregular past tense forms. \"Yesterday\" is a past time signal - all verbs in the sentence must match (past tense). go→went, buy→bought."},{"id":"g4eng-hd-012","q":"A pupil goes to the school library counter to borrow a book. Which sentence is the most polite?","opts":["Could I borrow this book, please?","Can I borrow this book, please?","Give me this book to borrow, please.","I want to borrow this book, please."],"a":"Could I borrow this book, please?","hint":"\"Please\" is in all four. Look at the word doing the asking instead.","exp":"\"Could\" makes the request softer than \"can\", and both are gentler than announcing what you want. Adding \"please\" to an order (\"give me\") does not turn the order into a request - the <b>asking word</b> does that."}],"g4eng-adjectives":[{"id":"g4eng-cover-adj-pic-06","q":"Which adjective best describes ❄️?","opts":["quickly","cold","run","tomorrow"],"a":"cold","hint":"An adjective tells us more about a noun.","exp":"<b>cold</b> is a describing word, so it is an adjective."},{"id":"g4eng-cover-adj-context-12","q":"Read: \"We watched a funny film.\" Which word is an adjective?","opts":["at","and","funny","the"],"a":"funny","hint":"Look for the word that describes a noun.","exp":"<b>funny</b> tells us more about a noun in the sentence."},{"id":"g4eng-hd-025","q":"A shopkeeper wants a sign for the quick queue at the till. Which sign is written correctly?","opts":["10 items or less","10 items or least","10 items or fewer","10 items or lesser"],"a":"10 items or fewer","hint":"You can count the items one by one. Does that change which word you need?","exp":"Things you can count take <b>fewer</b>: fewer items, fewer rupees, fewer pupils. Amounts you cannot count take \"less\": less water, less noise, less money. Many real shop signs get this wrong."},{"id":"g4eng-adj-009","q":"Which sentence correctly uses a SUPERLATIVE?","opts":["She is taller student in the class.","She is most tall student in the class.","She is the tallest student in the class.","She is the most tallest student in the class."],"a":"She is the tallest student in the class.","hint":"Superlative compares three or more things. Short adjectives → the + adjective + est. Never double the comparison.","exp":"\"<b>The tallest</b>\" is correct. Never write \"the most tallest\" - that is a double superlative error. Rule: short adjectives → the + -est (tallest). Long adjectives → the most + adjective (the most intelligent). The superlative always uses \"the\"."},{"id":"g4eng-adj-019","q":"Riya wrote: \"It was a hot, sunny day. The sea looked beautiful and calm. We felt very happy as we walked along the beach.\" How many ADJECTIVES appear in this passage?","opts":["6","5","7","4"],"a":"5","hint":"Find every word that describes a noun. Note: \"very\" is an adverb (it describes the adjective \"happy\"), not an adjective itself.","exp":"The 5 adjectives are: <b>hot</b> (day), <b>sunny</b> (day), <b>beautiful</b> (sea), <b>calm</b> (sea), <b>happy</b> (we - predicative adjective after \"felt\"). \"Very\" is an adverb that intensifies the adjective \"happy\". Counting adjectives in a passage is a key MIE Grade 4 skill."}]},"grade4-french":{"g4fr-vocabulaire":[{"id":"g4fr-cov-body-2","q":"Quel mot français désigne « eyes » ?","opts":["les oreilles","les yeux","la bouche","le nez"],"a":"les yeux","hint":"Choisis le mot français pour cette partie du corps.","exp":"La bonne réponse est <b>les yeux</b>."},{"id":"g4fr-voc-032","q":"Quel mot est un verbe (action) ?","opts":["frère","tête","chat","dormir"],"a":"dormir","hint":"Un verbe exprime une action ou un état.","exp":"\"<b>Dormir</b>\" est un verbe (to sleep) - c'est une action. <b>Tête</b> = nom (partie du corps), <b>frère</b> = nom (personne), <b>chat</b> = nom (animal). Les verbes répondent à la question \"Que fait-on ?\"."},{"id":"g4fr-plus-0088","q":"À quelle catégorie appartient « bleu » ?","opts":["une couleur","un verbe au passé","une question de maths","un nombre"],"a":"une couleur","hint":"Pense à ce que le mot désigne dans la vie réelle, pas à sa place dans la phrase.","exp":"« <b>bleu</b> » est lié à <b>une couleur</b>. Classer les mots aide la mémoire."},{"id":"g4fr-hd-002","q":"Le bus pour Curepipe part &agrave; <b>14 h 45</b>. Comment dit-on cette heure dans la langue de tous les jours ?","opts":["quatorze heures moins le quart","deux heures moins le quart","trois heures moins le quart","trois heures et quart"],"a":"trois heures moins le quart","hint":"Deux &eacute;tapes : passez d'abord de 14 h &agrave; l'heure de l'apr&egrave;s-midi, puis pensez aux 15 minutes qui manquent.","exp":"14 h = 2 heures de l'apr&egrave;s-midi, donc 15 h = 3 heures. &Agrave; 14 h 45 il manque un quart d'heure avant 15 h : on dit <b>trois heures moins le quart</b>."},{"id":"g4fr-hd-001","q":"Dans une boutique de Port-Louis, un touriste dit : <i>\"I would like to try this shirt.\"</i> Que dit-il en fran&ccedil;ais ?","opts":["Je voudrais essayer ce chemise.","Je voudrais goûter cette chemise.","Je voudrais essayer cette chemise.","J'essaie de vouloir cette chemise."],"a":"Je voudrais essayer cette chemise.","hint":"<i>to try</i> (un v&ecirc;tement) = essayer. <i>to taste</i> = go&ucirc;ter. Et <b>chemise</b> est f&eacute;minin.","exp":"On essaie un v&ecirc;tement et on go&ucirc;te un plat : ici c'est <b>essayer</b>. Comme <b>chemise</b> est f&eacute;minin, le d&eacute;monstratif est <b>cette</b>, jamais \"ce\"."}],"g4fr-noms":[{"id":"g4fr-plus-0267","q":"Quel est le pluriel de « l’amie » ?","opts":["des amie","la amies","le amies","les amies"],"a":"les amies","hint":"Au pluriel, l’article défini devient « les ». Regarde aussi la fin du nom.","exp":"Le pluriel est <b>les amies</b>. La plupart des noms prennent <b>-s</b>, mais certains mots ont une forme spéciale."},{"id":"g4fr-plus-0312","q":"Dans « un enfant », le nom est-il masculin ou féminin ?","opts":["un verbe","pluriel seulement","masculin","féminin"],"a":"masculin","hint":"L’article indéfini « un » indique le genre dans ce groupe nominal.","exp":"Dans <b>un enfant</b>, le nom est <b>masculin</b>. « un » est masculin et « une » est féminin : ici, c’est l’article qui donne le genre."},{"id":"g4fr-nom-009","q":"Choisis la phrase qui utilise les articles CORRECTEMENT.","opts":["La fille mange une pomme.","La garçon a un stylo.","Le fille mange une pomme.","Un école est grande."],"a":"La fille mange une pomme.","hint":"Vérifie le genre de chaque nom : fille (f.), garçon (m.), école (f.).","exp":"\"<b>La fille mange une pomme.</b>\" est correct. \"Fille\" est féminin → la fille ✓. \"Pomme\" est féminin → une pomme ✓. Les erreurs dans les autres : \"Le fille\" ✗ (fille est féminin), \"La garçon\" ✗ (garçon est masculin), \"Un école\" ✗ (école est féminin → une école ou l'école)."},{"id":"g4fr-nom-018","q":"Choisis la phrase où TOUS les articles sont correctement utilisés.","opts":["Les filles jouent avec le ballon rouge.","Le filles jouent avec les ballon rouge.","La filles jouent avec les ballons rouge.","Les fille jouent avec un ballons rouge."],"a":"Les filles jouent avec le ballon rouge.","hint":"Vérifie : \"filles\" = féminin pluriel, \"ballon\" = masculin singulier.","exp":"\"<b>Les filles jouent avec le ballon rouge.</b>\" - \"filles\" f.pl. → les filles ✓. \"ballon\" m.sg. → le ballon ✓. \"rouge\" avec ballon (m.sg.) → rouge ✓ (invariable). Les erreurs des autres options : mauvais genre, mauvais nombre ou accord incorrect."},{"id":"g4fr-hd-008","q":"Mettez toute la phrase au pluriel : <i>\"Le cheval saute par-dessus le vieux caillou.\"</i>","opts":["Les chevals sautent par-dessus les vieux cailloux.","Les chevaux saute par-dessus les vieilles cailloux.","Les chevaux sautent par-dessus les vieux caillous.","Les chevaux sautent par-dessus les vieux cailloux."],"a":"Les chevaux sautent par-dessus les vieux cailloux.","hint":"Trois choses changent : le nom en -al, le nom en -ou, et le verbe.","exp":"cheval &rarr; <b>chevaux</b> ; caillou &rarr; <b>cailloux</b> (bijou, caillou, chou, genou, hibou, joujou, pou prennent un -x) ; <b>vieux</b> ne change pas et le verbe passe au pluriel : <b>sautent</b>."}],"g4fr-verbes":[{"id":"g4fr-plus-0419","q":"Quelle phrase est correcte avec le verbe « aimer » ?","opts":["Nous aimes le chocolat.","Nous aimons le chocolat.","Je aimez le chocolat.","Ils aimons le chocolat."],"a":"Nous aimons le chocolat.","hint":"Le sujet « nous » demande la forme « nous » du verbe.","exp":"La bonne phrase est <b>Nous aimons le chocolat.</b> Vérifie toujours que le sujet et le verbe vont ensemble."},{"id":"g4fr-verb-025","q":"Quel adverbe de fréquence signifie \"always\" ?","opts":["jamais","souvent","toujours","parfois"],"a":"toujours","hint":"\"Always\" = sans exception.","exp":"\"Always\" = <b>toujours</b>. Adverbes de fréquence du plus au moins fréquent : <b>toujours</b> (always) → <b>souvent</b> (often) → <b>parfois</b> (sometimes) → <b>jamais</b> (never). \"Je ne mange <b>jamais</b> de bonbons.\" (jamais avec ne)."},{"id":"g4fr-verb-032","q":"Quelle phrase exprime une habitude avec \"souvent\" ?","opts":["Souvent je jouais hier.","Je jouerai souvent au football maintenant.","Je joue souvent au football le week-end.","Je jouais toujours jamais."],"a":"Je joue souvent au football le week-end.","hint":"\"Souvent\" + présent pour une habitude.","exp":"\"Je joue <b>souvent</b> au football le week-end.\" - <b>souvent</b> (often) + présent = habitude régulière. \"Le week-end\" confirme la régularité. Les autres options mélangent les temps de façon incorrecte."},{"id":"g4fr-verb-029","q":"Quelle phrase utilise \"parfois\" correctement ?","opts":["Je ne mange parfois jamais.","Je parfois mange des légumes.","Parfois = toujours.","Je mange parfois des légumes."],"a":"Je mange parfois des légumes.","hint":"\"Parfois\" se place après le verbe conjugué.","exp":"\"Je mange <b>parfois</b> des légumes.\" - <b>parfois</b> (sometimes) se place généralement après le verbe conjugué. Règle de position : sujet + verbe + <b>adverbe</b> + complément. On ne dit pas \"Je parfois mange\" (adverbe avant le verbe = anglicisme)."}]},"grade4-history":{"g4hist-locality":[{"id":"g4h-loc-007","q":"What is CONTINUITY in a locality?","opts":["The speed of change in a community","Something different from the past","Things that have NOT changed over time","A new building being built today"],"a":"Things that have NOT changed over time","hint":"Continuity means things have CONTINUED - stayed the same.","exp":"<b>Continuity</b> refers to things that have <b>stayed the same</b> over time. For example: an old church that is still standing after 100 years, or a traditional market that has existed for generations. Studying continuity helps us see which parts of our community have deep historical roots."},{"id":"g4h-loc-018","q":"Rajan's grandfather says: \"When I was young, the river was clean and children swam in it. Now it is polluted and nobody swims there.\" What type of CHANGE does this describe?","opts":["Negative change - the river got worse over time","No change - the river stayed the same","Political change - the government changed","Positive change - the river improved"],"a":"Negative change - the river got worse over time","hint":"Has the situation got better or worse? Is that a positive or negative change?","exp":"This describes a <b>negative change</b> - the river went from being clean and useable to polluted and unsafe. Not all change is positive; some changes in a locality can be harmful to the environment and community. Historians and geographers study both positive and negative changes when examining how a locality develops over time."},{"id":"g4h-loc-017","q":"Which of these would appear EARLIEST on a timeline of a Mauritian village?","opts":["Installation of electricity in homes","Arrival of the first settlers","Opening of a new supermarket","Construction of a concrete road"],"a":"Arrival of the first settlers","hint":"What happens FIRST in the history of any new community?","exp":"The <b>arrival of the first settlers</b> and building of initial homes would appear earliest on a timeline - this is how a community begins. Roads, electricity and supermarkets all come later as the community develops and grows. Understanding the order of events is the purpose of a timeline."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-007","q":"A grandmother says she used to fetch water from a river. Her grandchildren use a tap at home.<br>What has changed?","opts":["Water is now piped to houses","People need less water than before","The river has completely dried up","Children are not allowed near rivers"],"a":"Water is now piped to houses","hint":"The water still comes from somewhere. What changed is how it arrives.","exp":"The change is in <b>how water reaches people</b>, not in the water itself. Comparing two generations is a good way to see how daily life has changed."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-005","q":"Why do most towns in Mauritius have a market?","opts":["Markets are the oldest buildings in a town","Many people living nearby need to buy food","Markets are only built beside the sea","A town cannot be called a town without one"],"a":"Many people living nearby need to buy food","hint":"Think about who uses a market and where they come from.","exp":"A market appears where there are <b>enough customers</b> close by. Services grow where people are, which is why towns have more of them than villages."}],"g4hist-community":[{"id":"g4hg-cov-religions-5","q":"Why do people have different religions?","opts":["people have different beliefs","one religion is always right","all religions say the same things","only adults choose a religion"],"a":"people have different beliefs","hint":"Choose the respectful and accurate answer.","exp":"Families, histories and communities differ, so <b>people hold different beliefs</b> — and in Mauritius many live side by side."},{"id":"g4h-com-018","q":"Léa is Christian and her best friend Priya is Hindu. Priya invites Léa to celebrate Divali with her family. What is the BEST thing Léa can do?","opts":["Refuse to go because it is not her religion","Accept the invitation and join in respectfully","Ignore the invitation and avoid Priya at school","Attend but make fun of the traditions"],"a":"Accept the invitation and join in respectfully","hint":"Respecting other cultures means being open and curious, not fearful or dismissive.","exp":"The best response is to <b>accept the invitation and participate respectfully</b>. Learning about another community's festival shows <b>respect and curiosity</b>. Léa does not have to share Priya's religious beliefs to appreciate the beauty of Divali. In Mauritius, friends from different communities often celebrate each other's festivals - this builds friendship and national unity."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-018","q":"Why do people in Mauritius often wear light cotton clothes?","opts":["Heavy clothes are not allowed here","The climate is warm and humid all year","Cotton clothes never need washing","Cotton is the only cloth on the island"],"a":"The climate is warm and humid all year","hint":"Clothes suit the weather of a place.","exp":"Light cotton lets air through and keeps the wearer cool, which suits a <b>warm, humid climate</b>. What people wear is shaped by where they live."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-011","q":"A Mauritian child speaks Creole at home, learns English at school and studies French too.<br>What does that show about Mauritius?","opts":["English was the first language spoken here","Its history brought several languages together","Children must choose only one language","Creole is not a real language"],"a":"Its history brought several languages together","hint":"Each language arrived with different people at different times.","exp":"French rule, British rule and the people who came to work all left a language behind. Everyday life in Mauritius still shows that <b>history</b>."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-017","q":"A festival involves cleaning the house, wearing new clothes and lighting lamps.<br>What is the festival most likely about?","opts":["Remembering a sea voyage","A fresh start and light over darkness","The end of the sugar harvest","The building of a new road"],"a":"A fresh start and light over darkness","hint":"Cleaning, new clothes and lamps all suggest one idea.","exp":"Cleaning, new clothes and lamps all point to <b>renewal and light</b>. Working out meaning from customs is better than simply memorising a date."}],"g4hist-voyages":[{"id":"g4h-voy-007","q":"The DODO was a large flightless bird that was endemic to Mauritius. Why did the Dodo become EXTINCT?","opts":["It was taken to Europe and never came back","It flew away to another island far away","It was hunted by sailors and rats ate its eggs","It died from a very cold winter storm"],"a":"It was hunted by sailors and rats ate its eggs","hint":"Think about what changed when humans first arrived on Mauritius.","exp":"The <b>Dodo</b> became extinct (disappeared forever) because of humans arriving on Mauritius. Sailors <b>hunted Dodos for food</b> (they could not fly so they were easy to catch). Animals brought by humans - <b>rats, pigs and monkeys</b> - ate the Dodo's eggs, which were laid on the ground. Combined with <b>deforestation</b> (clearing forests), the Dodo's population collapsed. The last Dodo was seen around <b>1681</b>."},{"id":"g4h-voy-018","q":"The EBONY TREE is endemic to Mauritius and was nearly logged to extinction. Why is ebony so valuable?","opts":["Its hard, dark wood was prized for fine furniture","It can survive in salt water near the sea","It produces a fruit that is very sweet to eat","Its leaves are used to make a popular tea"],"a":"Its hard, dark wood was prized for fine furniture","hint":"Ebony is one of the hardest and darkest woods in the world.","exp":"<b>Ebony</b> (<i>Diospyros</i> species) is valuable because its wood is <b>extremely hard, dense and dark</b> - it was prized for making fine furniture, piano keys, musical instruments, and decorative carvings. When Dutch and French settlers arrived, ebony forests were massively logged for export. Today, Mauritian ebony is protected and conservation projects are working to restore ebony forests, particularly in Black River Gorges National Park."},{"id":"g4h-voy-010","q":"Why was the BLACK RIVER GORGES NATIONAL PARK established in Mauritius?","opts":["To create a racing circuit for cars","To grow more sugar cane for export","To build a new city for more people","To protect native forest and endemic species"],"a":"To protect native forest and endemic species","hint":"National parks protect nature - what does Mauritius need to protect most urgently?","exp":"<b>Black River Gorges National Park</b> (established 1994) covers about 6,574 hectares in the south-west of Mauritius. It protects <b>the last significant areas of native Mauritian forest</b> and the endemic species that live there - including the Echo Parakeet, Pink Pigeon, Mauritius Kestrel, and rare plants like the Ebony tree. It is vital for preserving Mauritius's unique natural heritage."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-026","q":"A ship carried live chickens and goats on a long voyage.<br>Why?","opts":["Animals were needed to pull the ropes","Fresh food could not be kept for months","The animals were being sold in Europe","The animals helped the sailors steer"],"a":"Fresh food could not be kept for months","hint":"There were no fridges. How do you keep meat fresh?","exp":"Without refrigeration, the only way to have fresh meat and eggs was to carry <b>living animals</b>. Old problems produced solutions that look strange today."},{"id":"g4hg-hd-021","q":"Ships stopped at Mauritius on their long voyages, though nothing was sold there.<br>Why was the island useful?","opts":["The island had spices to sell","Sailors could buy new ships there","Ships could take on fresh water and wood","It was the last stop before Europe"],"a":"Ships could take on fresh water and wood","hint":"What runs out on a ship after weeks at sea?","exp":"A ship far from land runs low on <b>water, food and timber</b> for repairs. The island was valuable for where it was, not for what it sold."}]},"grade4-maths":{"g4-numeration":[{"id":"g4m-num-055","q":"Round 73,642 to the nearest 1,000.","opts":["73,600","74,000","80,000","73,000"],"a":"74,000","hint":"Look at the hundreds digit. If it is 5 or more, round up the thousands.","exp":"The hundreds digit is 6 (≥ 5), so round up: 73,000 + 1,000 = <b>74,000</b>."},{"id":"g4m-num-058","q":"What number equals 50,000 + 4,000 + 200 + 60 + 9?","opts":["54,296","50,469","54,269","54,629"],"a":"54,269","hint":"Add each part: 50,000 + 4,000 = 54,000; + 200; + 60; + 9.","exp":"50,000 + 4,000 = 54,000; + 200 = 54,200; + 60 = 54,260; + 9 = <b>54,269</b>."},{"id":"g4m-num-059","q":"A number has 8 ten-thousands, 0 thousands, 5 hundreds, 1 ten and 4 units. What is the number?","opts":["85,014","80,154","80,514","80,541"],"a":"80,514","hint":"Write each digit in its correct position: ten-thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units.","exp":"8 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 1 (ten), 4 (units) → <b>80,514</b>."},{"id":"g4m-num-061","q":"A library has 23,754 books. During a fair, 1,250 more are donated. How many books are there now? Round the total to the nearest 1,000.","opts":["24,000","25,004","25,000","26,000"],"a":"25,000","hint":"First add the books, then round your answer.","exp":"23,754 + 1,250 = 25,004. The hundreds digit is 0 (< 5), so 25,004 rounds <b>down</b> to <b>25,000</b>."},{"id":"g4m-num-019","q":"Priya saves Rs 1,000 every month for 8 months. She then spends Rs 3,500. To the NEAREST HUNDRED RUPEES, how much does she have left?","opts":["Rs 5,000","Rs 3,500","Rs 4,500","Rs 4,000"],"a":"Rs 4,500","hint":"Step 1: 1,000 × 8 = total saved. Step 2: subtract 3,500. Step 3: round to nearest 100.","exp":"Total saved: Rs 1,000 × 8 = Rs 8,000. After spending: Rs 8,000 − Rs 3,500 = Rs 4,500. 4,500 is already a multiple of 100, so it rounds to <b>Rs 4,500</b>."}],"g4-four-ops":[{"id":"g4m-ops-055","q":"72,305 − 38,647 = ?","opts":["33,748","33,648","33,658","34,658"],"a":"33,658","hint":"Work right to left, borrowing from the next column where needed.","exp":"Borrow carefully column by column: result is <b>33,658</b>. Check: 33,658 + 38,647 = 72,305 ✓"},{"id":"g4m-ops-054","q":"46,873 + 37,459 = ?","opts":["84,332","84,322","84,342","83,332"],"a":"84,332","hint":"Work right to left and carry when a column sum reaches 10 or more.","exp":"3+9=12 (carry 1); 7+5+1=13 (carry 1); 8+4+1=13 (carry 1); 6+7+1=14 (carry 1); 4+3+1=8 → <b>84,332</b>."},{"id":"g4m-ops-018","q":"A minivan carries 8 passengers. How many minivans are needed to carry 63 people, with NO ONE left behind?","opts":["63","7","8","9"],"a":"8","hint":"63 ÷ 8 = 7 remainder 7. What does the remainder mean for the number of vans?","exp":"63 ÷ 8 = 7 remainder 7. Seven vans carry 56 people; 7 more still need to travel. One extra van is needed. Total: <b>8 vans</b>. When a remainder means someone is left out, always round UP the number of groups."},{"id":"g4m-ops-058","q":"Work out: 5 + 3 × 8 − 4","opts":["25","60","57","29"],"a":"25","hint":"Multiply and divide before adding and subtracting.","exp":"Do multiplication first: 3 × 8 = 24. Then left to right: 5 + 24 − 4 = <b>25</b>."},{"id":"g4m-ops-061","q":"A farmer packs 3,472 oranges into boxes of 8. He gives away 4 full boxes to his neighbours. How many oranges does he have left?","opts":["3,440","3,444","3,400","3,432"],"a":"3,440","hint":"How many oranges are in 4 boxes? Subtract that from the total.","exp":"4 boxes × 8 = 32 oranges given away. 3,472 − 32 = <b>3,440</b> oranges left. (All 3,472 fit exactly into 434 boxes with nothing left over.)"}],"g4-fractions":[{"id":"g4m-cov-freq-5","q":"Which fraction is equivalent to 7/8?","opts":["8/8","7/32","28/33","28/32"],"a":"28/32","hint":"Multiply the numerator and denominator by the same number.","exp":"Multiply the top AND the bottom by the same number, 4: 7 × 4 = 28 and 8 × 4 = 32. That gives <b>28/32</b>, which is the same amount as 7/8."},{"id":"g4m-frac-059","q":"1/3 of a number is 15. What is the number?","opts":["18","5","45","30"],"a":"45","hint":"Work backwards: if you know one part out of three, how do you find the whole?","exp":"1/3 × ? = 15 → ? = 15 × 3 = <b>45</b>."},{"id":"g4m-frac-058","q":"Arrange these fractions from smallest to largest: &nbsp; 5/9, &nbsp; 2/9, &nbsp; 8/9, &nbsp; 1/9","opts":["8/9, 5/9, 2/9, 1/9","1/9, 2/9, 5/9, 8/9","1/9, 5/9, 2/9, 8/9","2/9, 1/9, 5/9, 8/9"],"a":"1/9, 2/9, 5/9, 8/9","hint":"All denominators are the same - compare the numerators.","exp":"1 < 2 < 5 < 8, so the order from smallest to largest is <b>1/9, 2/9, 5/9, 8/9</b>."},{"id":"g4m-frac-019","q":"Asha walks 3/4 km to school and the same distance back home each day. Her brother walks 2 km in total each day. Who walks MORE, and by how much?","opts":["Her brother - he walks 0.5 km more than Asha","Her brother - he walks 1/4 km more","They walk the same distance","Asha - she walks 0.5 km more than her brother"],"a":"Her brother - he walks 0.5 km more than Asha","hint":"Asha's total = 3/4 + 3/4 = 6/4. Convert to a decimal or mixed number to compare with 2 km.","exp":"Asha: 3/4 + 3/4 = 6/4 = 1½ km = 1.5 km. Her brother: 2 km. Difference: 2 − 1.5 = 0.5 km. <b>Her brother walks 0.5 km more</b> than Asha. Converting 3/4 to 0.75 makes comparison easier."},{"id":"g4m-frac-061","q":"A recipe needs 3/4 cup of sugar. Priya makes the recipe 4 times. How many cups of sugar does she need altogether?","opts":["3 and 3/4 cups","2 cups","4 cups","3 cups"],"a":"3 cups","hint":"Multiply 3/4 by 4.","exp":"3/4 × 4 = 12/4 = <b>3 cups</b>."}]},"grade4-science":{"g4sci-living":[{"id":"g4s-liv-005","q":"The letters MRS GREN help us remember the characteristics of living things. What does the \"R\" stand for?","opts":["Reacting","Running","Respiration","Reproducing"],"a":"Respiration","hint":"MRS GREN is a mnemonic for the seven characteristics. Try to recall what each letter stands for, in order.","exp":"In MRS GREN, R stands for <b>Respiration</b> - living things breathe/take in air to release energy from food. MRS GREN: <b>M</b>ovement, <b>R</b>espiration, <b>S</b>ensitivity, <b>G</b>rowth, <b>R</b>eproduction, <b>E</b>xcretion, <b>N</b>utrition."},{"id":"g4s-liv-009","q":"Which statement is TRUE about ALL living things?","opts":["All living things live in the ocean","All living things produce waste","All living things have legs","All living things make their own food"],"a":"All living things produce waste","hint":"Excretion (E in MRS GREN) means removing waste. Which of these applies to ALL living things?","exp":"All living things <b>produce waste</b> (excretion). Animals produce waste like urine and carbon dioxide. Plants produce oxygen as a waste product of photosynthesis. Not all living things have legs, live in the ocean, or make their own food (animals eat, not make their food)."},{"id":"g4sc-liv-060","q":"A bat flies, is warm-blooded, has hair, and feeds its young on milk. To which group does the bat belong?","opts":["Reptiles, because it has wings","Mammals - it has hair and nurses its young","Insects, because it is small and flies","Birds, because it can fly"],"a":"Mammals - it has hair and nurses its young","hint":"Think about the key features of mammals - not just the ability to fly.","exp":"Despite being able to fly, the bat is a <b>mammal</b> because it has <b>hair, is warm-blooded, and nurses its young on milk</b>. Birds have feathers; insects have 6 legs and 3 body parts. The Rodrigues Fruit Bat is a famous mammal of Mauritius."},{"id":"g4sc-liv-061","q":"Neha finds an animal in her garden. It is cold-blooded, has moist skin (no scales), and lays its eggs in water. As a young animal it had gills, but now as an adult it has lungs. Which class does it belong to?","opts":["Fish - because it once had gills","Amphibian - it changes from gills to lungs","Bird - because it now breathes with lungs","Reptile - because it is cold-blooded"],"a":"Amphibian - it changes from gills to lungs","hint":"Which class goes through a change from a water-living larva (gills) to a land-living adult (lungs)?","exp":"The animal is an <b>amphibian</b> (most likely a frog or toad). Key clues: cold-blooded, moist skin (no scales), eggs laid in water, larva with gills → adult with lungs. Reptiles have scaly skin; fish keep their gills throughout life; birds are warm-blooded with feathers."},{"id":"g4sci-hd-001","q":"A car moves along the road, takes in fuel and gives off gases from its exhaust. A pupil says that makes it a living thing.<br>Why is she wrong?","opts":["It cannot grow or produce young of its own","It never moves without a driver inside it","It takes in fuel instead of taking in real food","It is made of metal, and metal is never alive"],"a":"It cannot grow or produce young of its own","hint":"Go through the seven life processes and find the ones a car can never do.","exp":"A living thing must do <b>all</b> the life processes, and a car can never grow bigger by itself or make a young car. The material it is made of does not decide the matter — living bodies contain metals too — and taking in fuel is close enough to nutrition to fool anyone who stops there."}],"g4sci-plants":[{"id":"g4s-plt-005","q":"Which conditions does a plant need to grow WELL?","opts":["Water, sunlight, air and warmth","Only water - nothing else","Darkness, cold temperature and no water","Sunlight and sand - no water needed"],"a":"Water, sunlight, air and warmth","hint":"Think about what a plant uses to make food and what it needs to stay alive.","exp":"Plants need <b>water</b> (absorbed by roots), <b>sunlight</b> (for photosynthesis), <b>air</b> (carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, oxygen for respiration), and <b>warmth</b> (for chemical reactions in the plant to work). Soil minerals/nutrients are also important for healthy growth."},{"id":"g4sc-dep-016","q":"Why are flowers brightly coloured and scented?","opts":["To hold the plant up","To store water","To attract insects","To frighten birds"],"a":"To attract insects","hint":"Insects carry pollen from flower to flower.","exp":"Bright colours and scent <b>attract insects</b>, which carry pollen between flowers."},{"id":"g4sc-dep-006","q":"A plant is left in a dark cupboard for two weeks. What is most likely to happen?","opts":["It makes more flowers","It turns pale and weak","It becomes darker green","It grows much faster"],"a":"It turns pale and weak","hint":"It cannot make food without light.","exp":"Without sunlight the plant cannot make food, so <b>it turns pale and weak</b> and may die."},{"id":"g4sc-dep-017","q":"Some seeds have tiny hairs and are carried away by the wind. Why is this useful?","opts":["They stop other seeds growing","They grow faster in the air","They grow away from the parent","They become much heavier"],"a":"They grow away from the parent","hint":"Think about crowding around one plant.","exp":"Being carried away means <b>they grow away from the parent plant</b>, where there is more light, water and space."},{"id":"g4sci-hd-074","q":"Two bean seeds are planted. One is put on a sunny windowsill and watered; the other is put in a dark cupboard and watered.<br>The cupboard seed sprouts and grows tall and pale, then dies. Why did it grow at all?","opts":["The seed took food out of the soil around it","The seed had its own food store to start with","Cupboards hold a small amount of hidden light","Water alone can feed a plant for a few weeks"],"a":"The seed had its own food store to start with","hint":"Something inside the seed keeps it going before the first leaves work.","exp":"A seed carries a <b>food store</b> that powers the first growth. Once that runs out the plant must make its own food, and without light it cannot, so it dies."}],"g4sci-animals":[{"id":"g4s-ani-005","q":"A HUMAN is an example of an OMNIVORE. What does this mean?","opts":["Humans eat only meat","Humans eat only vegetables","Humans eat both plants and animals","Humans do not need food"],"a":"Humans eat both plants and animals","hint":"Omni- means \"all\" - omnivores eat from all food types.","exp":"An <b>omnivore</b> eats <b>both plants and animals</b>. Humans eat rice, vegetables, fruit (plants) AND fish, chicken, eggs (animals). Other omnivores: bears, pigs, foxes, crows."},{"id":"g4sc-ani-052","q":"Which food chain is in the CORRECT order?","opts":["Insect → grass → frog → hawk → snake","Hawk → snake → frog → insect → grass","Frog → grass → insect → snake → hawk","Grass → insect → frog → snake → hawk"],"a":"Grass → insect → frog → snake → hawk","hint":"Food chains always start with a plant (producer). Energy flows from left to right.","exp":"The correct order is: <b>Grass → Insect → Frog → Snake → Hawk</b>. Grass is the producer; the insect (primary consumer) eats grass; the frog (secondary consumer) eats the insect; the snake (tertiary consumer) eats the frog; the hawk (top predator) eats the snake. Energy flows from the Sun through the chain."},{"id":"g4s-ani-018","q":"In Mauritius: Sugar cane → Rat → Barn Owl. If BARN OWL numbers increase a lot, what will happen to the RAT population?","opts":["Rat numbers will increase because owls protect them","Rats will move to another island","Rat numbers will fall as more owls eat them","Rat numbers will stay the same"],"a":"Rat numbers will fall as more owls eat them","hint":"More predators (owls) eating the prey (rats) - what happens to prey numbers?","exp":"More owls → more rats hunted → <b>rat numbers decrease</b>. This is why barn owls are used as natural pest control in Mauritius sugar cane fields - they keep the rat population down without chemicals. This is called <b>biological control</b>."},{"id":"g4s-ani-009","q":"Which food chain is CORRECT for a Mauritius forest ecosystem?","opts":["Grass → Rabbit → Eagle","Grass → Eagle → Rabbit","Eagle → Rabbit → Grass","Rabbit → Grass → Eagle"],"a":"Grass → Rabbit → Eagle","hint":"A food chain always starts with a producer (plant). Energy flows from left to right.","exp":"The correct order is <b>Grass → Rabbit → Eagle</b>. Food chains always start with a <b>producer</b> (plant) which makes its own food. Energy flows: plant → herbivore → carnivore. The arrows show the direction of energy flow (who eats whom)."},{"id":"g4sci-hd-075","q":"A fish from the lagoon is put into a bucket of fresh tap water and soon dies.<br>Why, when both are water?","opts":["A bucket is too small for a fish to live","Tap water is far too cold for any fish","Its body is suited only to salty water","Fish can only breathe in moving water"],"a":"Its body is suited only to salty water","hint":"Water is not just water. What is dissolved in it matters.","exp":"A lagoon fish is adapted to <b>salty surroundings</b> and cannot cope in fresh water. A habitat is defined by its conditions, not by the substance alone."}]}}};
